WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.289
Chunking turns source material into retrievable pieces, but it is not a neutral formatting step.

2
00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:20.401
The wrong chunking strategy can remove context, mix unrelated topics, split requirements from exceptions, separate a table from its explanation, or detach a statement from the source rules that control who may see it.

3
00:00:20.401 --> 00:00:23.393
Good chunking follows structure and meaning.

4
00:00:23.393 --> 00:00:32.443
Headings, sections, lists, tables, version notes, policy clauses, and natural topic boundaries should influence how content is divided.

5
00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:38.502
A chunk should be small enough to retrieve precisely, but large enough to keep useful context.

6
00:00:38.502 --> 00:00:46.095
When a document contains exceptions or conditions, those details should not be separated from the rule they qualify.

7
00:00:46.095 --> 00:00:48.396
Metadata makes chunks governable.

8
00:00:48.396 --> 00:01:02.585
Useful fields include source document ID, title, owner, classification, tenant, section heading, reference URL, ingestion date, modified date, effective date, expiration date, lifecycle status, and permissions.

9
00:01:02.585 --> 00:01:07.340
These fields support filtering, ranking, review, and eventual cleanup.

10
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:14.396
Provenance answers the practical questions: where did this come from, who may see it, and is it still current?

11
00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:18.922
Citations and source references are not just user-interface polish.

12
00:01:18.922 --> 00:01:24.137
They support accountability, correction, expert review, and user confidence.

13
00:01:24.137 --> 00:01:28.049
Without provenance, a good answer can still be hard to trust.

14
00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:35.718
Private RAG architecture should keep every chunk attached to its source context, lifecycle status, and access rules.

15
00:01:35.718 --> 00:01:44.615
The retrieval layer should be able to explain not only what text was selected, but also why that text was eligible for the user and workflow.

16
00:01:44.615 --> 00:01:46.839
Chunking also needs maintenance.

17
00:01:46.839 --> 00:02:00.185
As documents are revised, renamed, merged, or retired, derived chunks should be refreshed so the index does not preserve old fragments that no longer match the source record or the current permission model.
